From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] On the variable refresh path, the launch owns synchronization

PortareOS runs every game on the variable refresh path unless black frame
insertion asks for fixed refresh; runemu exports MESA_VK_WSI_DISPLAY_VRR=1
for it, which Mesa and setsettings read as well. There frames are timed at
the core's rate with vsync off (0018, 0019) and the display driver repeats
them below the panel's range. Turning vsync on, or Sync to Exact Content
Framerate off, in the menu or with the hotkey, or through a saved
override, left that path for the session or for every launch.

On that path drivers_init decides from the content's rate alone whether
it fits twice into the display's rate (0019's check) and the answer is
kept for the content. After the config loads, after an override is
loaded or unloaded, and in drivers_init, both settings are set from it:
vsync off and Sync to Exact Content Framerate on, or the reverse for
content locked to the display. Loading or unloading an override while
the content runs rereads both from the files without reinitialising the
drivers; without the kept answer that undid the lock. Both entries leave
the menu (Video > Synchronization, Latency, Frame Throttle), and the
hotkey does nothing. With the variable unset or 0 nothing changes.

Signed-off-by: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
---
diff --git a/configuration.c b/configuration.c
--- a/configuration.c
+++ b/configuration.c
@@ -6040,6 +6040,52 @@
 #ifdef HAVE_CONFIGFILE
    config_parse_file(global);
 #endif
+   config_vrr_path_owns_sync(config_st);
+}
+
+/* PortareOS runs every game on the variable refresh path unless black
+ * frame insertion asks for fixed refresh, and runemu says which by
+ * exporting MESA_VK_WSI_DISPLAY_VRR, which Mesa and setsettings read too.
+ * On that path the launch owns synchronization: frames are timed at the
+ * core's rate (Sync to Exact Content Framerate, 0018) with vsync off, and
+ * the display driver repeats them below the panel's range. Neither the
+ * menu, the hotkey nor a saved override turns vsync on there.
+ *
+ * Content that does not fit twice into the panel's rate is locked to the
+ * display instead (0019): vsync on, Sync to Exact Content Framerate off.
+ * drivers_init decides that from the content's rate and it holds for the
+ * content. It is kept here, not only in the two settings: loading or
+ * unloading an override while the content runs rereads both from the
+ * files without reinitialising the drivers, which would leave the running
+ * drivers locked and the runloop pacing at the core's rate. */
+static bool config_vrr_display_lock = false;
+
+bool config_vrr_path(void)
+{
+   const char *v = getenv("MESA_VK_WSI_DISPLAY_VRR");
+   return v && v[0] == '1' && v[1] == '\0';
+}
+
+void config_vrr_set_display_lock(bool lock)
+{
+   config_vrr_display_lock = lock;
+}
+
+void config_vrr_path_owns_sync(settings_t *settings)
+{
+   bool vsync, runloop;
+   if (!settings || !config_vrr_path())
+      return;
+   vsync   = config_vrr_display_lock;
+   runloop = !config_vrr_display_lock;
+   if (     settings->bools.video_vsync        != vsync
+         || settings->bools.vrr_runloop_enable != runloop)
+      RARCH_LOG("[Config] Variable refresh: vsync %s, Sync to Exact Content Framerate %s%s.\n",
+            vsync   ? "on" : "off",
+            runloop ? "on" : "off",
+            config_vrr_display_lock ? ", locked to the display" : "");
+   settings->bools.video_vsync        = vsync;
+   settings->bools.vrr_runloop_enable = runloop;
 }
 
 #ifdef HAVE_CONFIGFILE
